\section{Introduction and state of the art}

In "Zen and the Art of Genetic Algorithms", \cite{Zen}, Goldberg lays out a series of {\em koans} or principles for nature-inspired metaheuristics: behind "Let nature be your guide", which goes first, and "Trust implicit parallelism" goes "The Middle Way": "Balance exploration and exploitation". This balance of exploration and exploitation has been, since then, one of the founding principles of metaheuristic design, implying that you need to have operators that combine individuals with high fitness, as well as others that "break" them moving the population to new, random areas of the search space; this also follows the 4th principle, "Do not fear destruction".

This has been widely understood as the need to balance the application rate of these two kinds of operators. However, {\em balancing} is not a simple thing; in practice, there's a single point where a system reaches  balance; {\em a priori}, one would think that striking balance is well-nigh impossible. Furthermore, there is not a consensus on {\em where} exploration and exploitation actually occur \cite{eiben98}, other than admitting that mutation is almost purely an exploration operator. In many cases, the nature of an operator is related to its action on {\em schemata}, or blocks that perform better than average; {\em exploitative} operators keep these schemata, while {\em explorative} disrupt or create new ones. There are some issues with this definition, including the fact that in most cases the algorithm will be working on real-valued chromosomes where the concept of schemata is not so helpful.

This lack of formal definition of the two terms, or precision about where they occur, certainly does not help \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} gather insights into the inner workings of algorithms or exactly where the core of their success lies. In general, a rule of thumb indicates that if evolution is stagnated, there is too much exploration of parts of the search space that are far away from the solutions; on the other hand, if diversity is high, exploitation
% do you mean exploration? exploitation will mean less diversity. - Mario
% No, exploitation; I've explained it a bit.
is working correctly, because it is generating individuals that sample different parts of the search space without falling into a local minimum; this is why, in general, experiments that try to examine this balance tend to work with diversity, measured as fitness or chromosome space entropy. \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} surveys different ways of controlling and maintaining diversity using different techniques such as learning in order to keep this balance.

There are two issues with these rules of thumb. While it is true that lack of diversity is a symptom of stagnation, high diversity might be caused by excessive exploration, that is, generation of new individuals in unpromising areas of the search space, as well as good exploitation, generation of a sufficient amount of high-fitness individuals in promising areas. In practice, there is no way of distinguishing {\em where} these new, different individuals are created and if the area of the search space they occupy is promising or not through a simple measure such as diversity.

This leaves researchers with a methodological gap: overall, when new metaheuristic algorithms are designed and new operators are introduced, they can only follow certain rules of thumb, like measuring diversity \cite{HASHIM2019646,merelo2022brave-anon} to ensure that there is a correct balance between exploration and exploitation, and even with high diversity, fitness might not reach an optimum value.

This is why in this paper we propose a new methodology for investigating the exploration-exploitation balance: using the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell (FWL) \cite{Lovell01121963} theorem to model the relationship between algorithm parameters and fitness, mediated by the number of evaluations. We can agree that better algorithms will lead to better fitness with fewer evaluations. At the end of the day, what we pursue with algorithm design is precisely that: obtaining efficient algorithms from the computational (and also energetic) point of view. A statistical model of fitness mediated by the number of evaluations and generations
% The evaluations-mediated model? remove the (), number of generations is included, but is only a factor. - Mario
% OK, clarified a bit
will help us find out how much fitness improvement comes from more exploration (more evaluations) or better algorithm adjustment through different parameters. Although this is not exactly a clear exploration/exploitation division, in practice, through parameter adjustment and how they act on the population (exploring, exploiting, or both at the same time), we can try to understand better how these mechanisms work, and adjust the algorithms so that they leverage these insights.

We will apply this model to different design and parameter choices in the Brave New Algorithm \cite{merelo2022brave-anon}, a stratified-population memetic algorithm that was designed with the EEB in mind. For this reason, we need to assess the overall and parametric design in order to ascertain how the number of evaluations influences the achieved result in terms of fitness. We will use the model to gather insights on the exploration and exploitation mechanisms and how they interact or counteract each other.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows: next we present
background and previous work in Section \ref{sec:soa}; then, we proceed to
describe the experimental design (Section \ref{sec:bna}), followed by the experimental results
(Section \ref{sec:exp});
finally, we discuss these results and draw our conclusions in Section \ref{sec:conclusions}.

\section{State of the art}\label{sec:soa}

From the initial paper by Eiben and Schippers \cite{eiben98}, and despite having been a tenet of evolutionary computation from the beginning, there is not an extensive literature that look at the so-called exploration/exploitation balance. In general, they are seen as opposing forces that need to be kept in balance. \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} surveys how they are done at a low, operator, level, how they are controlled, and how the balance is actually achieved. It is not the final word on the subject; for instance, it states that more research is needed to understand how the different operators contribute to exploration/exploitation, and ask for more direct measures. In general, it opens several paths to understanding better its interaction with representation, how different phases of search affect it and eventually how effective any measure of that balance can be applied to improvement of search. In general, it leaves more question open than it closes, even as it establishes diversity as the best way to represent EEB and, eventually, to control it.

Recent approaches have tackled the problem differently, however. An article by Ferhat et al. \cite{ferhat2025measures} focus mainly on measures of diversity and convergence rates; however, these are at most indirect measures that do not really reflect how exploration and exploitation affect fitness improvement. A novel measure of exploration and exploration is proposed in \cite{math14091406} where exploitation is intra-peak motion in search space, and exploitation between peak. This is also applied to other algorithms \cite{math14173119}, but even if this is an operative way of understanding these measures, it needs to assume and model search space in order to apply them; in this search space models such as the one proposed in \cite{laredo20} can help, providing more efficient sampling methods for what they call {\em exploitation}.

In this paper, however, we will try to attack the problem of optimizing the improvement of fitness per individual evaluation, without the reference to specific search space models or even to a balance between two opposing forces.

\section{A brave new algorithm} \label{sec:bna}

Far from trying to obscure the meaning of a metaheuristic by using a metaphor \cite{sorensen}, our description of a Brave New Algorithm (BNA), based in the novel Brave New World, by Aldous Huxley \cite{huxley2022brave}, searches to simplify the explanation of a stratified evolutionary algorithm (EA) by giving every {\em stratum} a name that is relatively easy to understand for those familiar with this book.
Thus, we are not so much proposing a new metaheuristic as a new way of structuring the population and the reproduction process, which can be applied to any metaheuristic that uses a population of individuals, in such a way that recombination of individuals and random generation is performed in a different way by each stratum.

Our stated objective is to try and find an easier way to disentangle exploration from exploration by keeping some layers explicitly exploratory and some of them either more balanced or simply exploiting; in practice, this means that mutation and crossover will be distributed in a different way depending on the layer or {\em stratum}.
The algorithm is described in more detail in \cite{merelo2022brave-anon,bna25-anon}, but we will give a brief description here for the sake of completeness.

What BNA introduces with respect to other EAs is the division of the population into {\em castes} (i.e., strata);
each receiving a differentiated treatment for reproduction.
More precisely, reproduction goes as follows by caste:
\begin{itemize}
\item The $\alpha$ caste will mate only with other individuals of the same caste, undergoing (possibly) mutation and then crossover. Since this stratum is mainly recombining high-fitness individuals, it is mainly a {\em exploitative} one {\em a priori}.
\item The $\beta$ caste will reproduce only with members of the $\alpha$ caste, again with mutation and crossover. In this case, the exploitative element is more important than the explorative.
\item The $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ castes will only reproduce via cloning and mutation, so they are purely explorative.
\item The $\gamma$ caste will use hill-climbing for a number of steps to improve its fitness. In a EEB framework, it is difficult to qualify this stratum; although it is sampling the space randomly, it will stop when fitness is no longer improving. The issues with pigeonholing some operators this way is one of the reasons why we are looking at the fitness/evaluations relationship from a different angle.
\end{itemize}

Mutation and crossover rates can be established independently for every caste, although by default all mutation rates will be the same.

All individuals will be ranked according to their fitness and then assigned to the castes;
in particular, the $\alpha$ caste will receive the best while the $\gamma$ caste will get only the last ones; percentages assigned to every caste will be an algorithm parameter, although in order to simplify we will only change the $\alpha$ percentage, with $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ getting always 5\%; $\beta$ is always double that of $\alpha$ and finally $\gamma$ the rest; this gives us a single parameter to regulate this aspect of the algorithm, which is strongly related to the exploration/exploitation balance. Selection, usually considered a {\em exploitative} operator, would still be acting here; however, it also selects which operators are going to be applied by placing individuals in different strata, some of them purely explorative. Again, it is difficult to place it in a EEB framework.

The algorithm uses elitism to keep the best member of the previous population
(replacing the worst one) but other than that, all individuals have offspring one way or the other. This is the main difference with respect to other EAs: the worst elements are not dropped from the reproduction pool but mutated in a random or directed way (in the case of $\gamma$). This is intended to keep a diverse population, striking a good exploration/exploitation balance again. This diversity can be enhanced by using differential mutation rates for the different castes;
while
this adds another parameter for experimental design,
it allows customization for specific problems or problem sizes.

The free parameters are thus:
\begin{itemize}
\item Population size:
Note that all castes need to have a non-zero integer size, which limits the possible values for this parameter due to the relationship between the population size for $\alpha$ and $\beta$ caste.
\item $\alpha$ percentage: {\em a priori}
the essential parameter for controlling the balance between exploitation and exploration.
\item Mutation rates
(for all castes except $\gamma$): these can be set to the same value or customized for every caste.
\item Local-search depth: maximum number of hill-climbing {\em steps} while there is still improvement in the fitness.
\item Stopping criterion: the maximum number of generations without improvement, that is, {\em max\_gens}.
\end{itemize}

Note that the orchestrated use of local improvement within this general evolutionary engine makes BNA fall within the general scope of memetic algorithms \cite{cotta2025harnessing}. %
The algorithm is written in Julia \cite{perkel2019julia} and released as open source \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}. Julia is a language designed with a JIT compiler to be fast and introduces native concurrent execution. It has been already used in a number of evolutionary algorithm implementations such as \cite{sanchez2023evolp}. In our case, this parallel execution feature matches well the multi-caste evolution of
BNA, opening new venues of research as well as energy-saving. In this paper, however, we are still profiling and optimizing at the single-processor level.

\section{Experiments and results}\label{sec:exp}

We have used the an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X 16-Core Processor running Ubuntu 25.10 with kernel {\tt 6.17.0-19-generic}. The experiments will be run from a Perl script, capturing output from {\sf pinpoint} and saving it to a CSV file. These files are available from GitHub and also from this paper's repository. We make 30 executions for every parameter combination. For every experiment, we save the following data:\begin{itemize}
\item Algorithm parameters: {\tt max\_gens}, that is, maximum number of generations without change before stopping, {\tt steps}, the maximum number of hill-climbing steps in the $\gamma$ caste, {\tt alpha}, which is the percentage of individuals that are going to be placed in the $\alpha$ caste, and {\tt population\_size}.
\item Number of generations that have been actually run
\item Number of evaluations, which include also those made during the hillclimbing step.
\end{itemize}

Please take into account that this is an algorithm-driven, not fixed budget, termination condition. When the population is stagnated, it will no longer improve fitness, which forces the algorithm to stop via the {\em max\_gen} condition.

% We have established another experimental condition, specific for multi-die CPUs, which tries to minimize the effect of temperature on energy consumption: at the beginning of every run, die temperature is checked and (using {\tt taskset}) the process is always sent to the {\em hottest} die \cite{hot-first-anon} - not relevant in this paper

%For workload runs we capture the following data, additionally to the operating data mentioned above: Fitness of the best individual at the end of the run ad the total number of evaluations <and generations.

For this paper, we have focused on a single problem and a single dimension: The Sphere floating-point fitness function included in the BBOB benchmark \cite{hansen2010comparing} for 10 dimensions. The reason we have not introduced more functions and dimensions is that we wanted to focus on the effect of the operating conditions (measured through running time and temperatures) and the algorithm parameters as well as results (fitness, evaluations). Introducing more degrees of freedom would not give us more insights into this model, and it might confuse the interpretation of results\footnote{In the discussion section we will include how these results can be generalized to other problems and dimensions.}. Statistical analysis, however, provides the certainty we need to draw sound conclusions from this set of experiments.
%%Streamlines paragraph by moving an aside remark to a footnote -Carlos

Our intention is to try, with experiments, to see the dependence of energy consumption on algorithm parameters as well as operating conditions, and the dependence of fitness reached on the latter, in order to check if it is possible at all to reach lower levels of fitness spending less or the same energy. The four parameters that we will be changing are:\begin{itemize}
\item $\alpha$ percentage with two values: 10\% and 25\%. Since it is the main lever for the exploitation/exploration balance, we are looking for better fitness values with less space exploration (less evaluations).
\item The number or {\sf steps} for hill-climbing in the $\gamma$ caste is related to
exploitation. %% local search is an intensifying operator, so more steps means more fitness-oriented exploitation.
We will test 16 and 64 steps.
\item The number of generations without improvement to stop or {\sf max\_gens} will be set to 10 and 25; more generations allow more exploration and the possibility to go up a notch in fitness.
\item Population size is going to be set to 400 and 800; larger population means more diversity giving it a better chance to exploit existing values, but also more exploration.
\end{itemize}

This gives us 8 different parameter settings times 30 $W$ and 31 $B$ runs. To ensure we cover different operating conditions, we repeat every set 4 times after at least one reboot; the distance between one set of experiments and the next is random.
We have an open science policy and therefore, all code, experimental results and analyses are available under a free license at our repository \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}.

The first batch experiments will be used as baseline; the mutation rate for every caste will be 40\%, meaning that, on average, 4 components of every individual mutated will change randomly within a certain range; using this baseline, we will perform different experiments changing algorithm or parameters.

<<ppsn.low.mutation, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Comparison of energy consumed vs. final temperature for the microopt and low mutation set of experiments", fig.height=4, fig.pos="!tbp">>=
source("R/process_deltas.R")
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(ggExtra) # for ggMarginal

load("data/ppsn_low_mutation.rds")
load("data/ppsn_microopt.rds") # redo this, we can use the die now

ppsn_low_mutation$group <- "low_mutation"
ppsn_microopt$group <- "microopt"

comparison_microopt_low_mutation <- rbind(ppsn_low_mutation, ppsn_microopt)
comparison_microopt_low_mutation[ startsWith( comparison_microopt_low_mutation$work, "base-"),]$work <- "Base"
comparison_microopt_low_mutation[ !startsWith( comparison_microopt_low_mutation$work, "Base"),]$work <- "Workload"

comparison_microopt_low_mutation %>% group_by( work, group, die ) %>% summarise( count = n(), .groups="drop" ) -> comparison_microopt_low_mutation_summary

comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp <- comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp_2 / 2
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp <- comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp_1 + comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp_2 / 2

comparison_microopt_low_mutation %>% group_by( work, group, die ) %>%
  summarise( mean_initial_temp = mean(initial_temp),
             mean_final_temp = mean(final_temp),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             mean_seconds = mean(seconds),
             .groups="drop" ) -> comparison_microopt_low_mutation_operating_conditions_summary

comparison_microopt_low_mutation$delta_temp <- comparison_microopt_low_mutation$final_temp - comparison_microopt_low_mutation$initial_temp

ggplot(comparison_microopt_low_mutation, aes(x = final_temp, y = PKG, color=group )) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  facet_wrap(~work) +
  labs(x = "Final Temperature",
    y = "PKG Energy"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

@

After this first optimization at the framework layer, we will make an additional change that concerns the algorithm itself. While the mutation rate was set at 40\%, in this case, we will lower it to 10\%, meaning that when changing a chromosome, only one element of the chromosome will be changed. While this might seem an obvious way of reducing random search,
(i.e., exploration),
what we are interested in is the way it will affect the operating conditions first, and then obviously the energy consumed as well as the fitness reached. This is why we look first, in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.low.mutation}, at the energy spent in $W$ and $B$ runs (in two panels) by the new baseline (the previously analyzed microoptimization version) and this one, called {\em low mutation}. We use the final temperature (taken as the average of the temperature of the two dies) as the $x$ axis, because it will depend on the operating conditions, the general change brought by the previous runs of the same parameters or similar, and obviously the experiment; the $y$ axis is the total PKG energy. We can observe the difference between the two groups, but what is interesting here is that the final temperature on the $B$ runs, which in principle depends only on population size, is lower for the microopt group. This indicates that the energy spent for the $B$ runs is {\em covariant} with the amount spent by the $W$ runs While subtracting the baseline runs from the workload runs to obtain workload-only energy might be useful for reducing the inter-quartile range and eliminate a part of the framework overhead, what we see here is that we need to take $B$ into the model in order to compute the dependence; it might be more complicated than the average, with one having more weight than other, or simply less than the average, which would compromise the actual value of the workload.

In order to take that into account, we will perform a sequential series of models, residualizing several parts of the complex model, so that we can have a more accurate picture of the dependence of energy on energy parameters and, especially, on the change of the values that we have performed here.

<<ppsn.low.mutation.model, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Model for the low mutation change.", fig.height=4, fig.pos="!tbp">>=
low_mutation_group_temp_model <- glm( initial_temp ~ group + work, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$residual_initial_temp <- residuals( low_mutation_group_temp_model )

low_mutation_group_final_temp_model <- glm( delta_temp ~ group + work + initial_temp + seconds, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation$residual_delta_temp <- residuals( low_mutation_group_final_temp_model )

source("R/process_covariates.R")

comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates <- process_covariates( comparison_microopt_low_mutation )

low_mutation_time_model <- glm( seconds ~ seconds_baseline_prev + seconds_baseline_post + group + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates$residual_seconds <- residuals( low_mutation_time_model )

low_mutation_energy_model <- glm( PKG ~ group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

anova_low_mutation_energy_model <- anova( low_mutation_energy_model )

anova_low_mutation_energy_model_table <- data.frame( term = rownames(anova_low_mutation_energy_model),
           F_value = anova_low_mutation_energy_model$F,
           p_value = anova_low_mutation_energy_model$`Pr(>F)`)

anova_low_mutation_energy_model_table  %>%
  filter( !is.na(F_value) ) %>%
  filter ( p_value < 0.05 ) %>%
  arrange( desc(F_value) ) %>%
  head(10) %>%
  mutate( percentage_variance = F_value / sum(F_value, na.rm=T) * 100 ) -> anova_low_mutation_energy_model_top10

anova_low_mutation_energy_model_top10$F_value <- NULL
anova_low_mutation_energy_model_top10$p_value <- NULL

kable(anova_low_mutation_energy_model_top10, caption = "Top 10 coefficients in the ANOVA model for the low mutation change",  format = "latex" ) %>%
  kable_styling( full_width = F, position = "center" )

low_mutation_energy_interaction_model <- glm( PKG ~ PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + group*evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )

# compare these two models using AIC
model_comparison <- AIC( low_mutation_energy_model, low_mutation_energy_interaction_model )

evaluations_model <- glm( evaluations ~ group + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )
comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates$residual_evaluations <- residuals( evaluations_model )

low_mutation_energy_mediated_model <- glm( PKG ~ group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_delta_temp + residual_seconds + residual_evaluations + alpha*steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates )
@

Table \ref{tab:ppsn.low.mutation.model} shows the percentage of variance explained by the top 10 significant terms in the model, where we have residualized initial temperature, delta, and seconds. Using GLM, we create a model that includes all variables as well as the baseline PKG values, which would account for the framework overhead. Indeed, the previous $B$ PKG value is the top variable explaining the variance. We can already query this model about how much energy we are saving or not with this model; the coefficient is
\Sexpr{round(low_mutation_energy_model$coefficients["groupmicroopt"], 2)}; all things being equal, this is the saving in Joules you will get on average if we do not apply this change. This is a small quantity, however; we must consider that there is a relatively small dependence on the number of evaluations (\Sexpr{signif(low_mutation_energy_model$coefficients["evaluations"], digits=2)}) that, however, is significant only at the \Sexpr{round(summary(low_mutation_energy_model)$coefficients["evaluations","Pr(>|t|)"], 3)} level.

<<ppsn.low.mutation.fitness.table, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Fitness vs evaluations, colored by PKG energy spend in Joules", fig.height=4, fig.pos="!tbp">>=
comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates %>% group_by( population_size, steps, max_gens, alpha, group ) %>%
  summarise( mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness),
             trim_mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness, trim=0.2),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             trim_mean_PKG = mean(PKG, trim=0.2),
             mean_evaluations = mean(evaluations) ) -> summary_comparison_low_mutation
ggplot(comparison_microopt_low_mutation_covariates, aes(x = evaluations, y = diff_fitness, shape=group,color=PKG)) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  scale_y_log10() +
  scale_color_gradient(low = "blue", high = "red") +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5,size=3) +
  labs(y = "Diff Fitness",
    x = "Evaluations"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()
@

So, a price had to be paid for this lunch, although it was not an expensive one. The question turns out to be whether we managed to achieve better fitness in the process, to compensate for this price.
In Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.low.mutation.fitness.table} we plot with different shapes the new baseline, {\sf microopt}, vs. the current {\sf low\_mutation} which uses circles, using log-scaled evaluations as $x$ axis and log-scaled difference to optimum fitness ({\sf diff\_fitness}) as $y$; the color scale stands for PKG energy in joules. There are two distinct {\em clouds} for every shape: the triangle ({\sf microopt}) cloud starts to the left, that is, fewer evaluations, and on top, worse {\sf diff\_fitness}. There is an issue with this block: the slope is very small, that is, more evaluations achieve only a small improvement in fitness. On the other hand, that does not happen with the {\sf low\_mutation} cluster: while
it is slightly redder, more evaluations and more energy expenses, much better fitness is effectively achieved.
% Check the sentence above -Carlos

The model presented above shows that evaluations are not the only factor in energy expenses, and that there are non-linear effects on the operating conditions that cause that, for a similar number of evaluations, {\sf microopt} spends less energy, but at the end of the day what matters is fitness, so we can also look for a certain hue in this graph and see that the fitness obtained for a certain energy expense is always much lower. In general, this graph shows that the {\em low\_mutation} spends more energy because it is running for more evaluations, and this happens because it is able to maintain a high diversity in the population, not getting stuck at a value for {\tt max\_gens} and thus stopping.

<<ppsn.cero.alpha, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Comparison of energy consumed vs. final temperature for the microopt and zero alpha set of experiments", fig.height=3, fig.pos="!tbp">>=
load("data/ppsn_no_alpha.rds")
ppsn_no_alpha$group <- "no_alpha"
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha <- rbind(ppsn_no_alpha, ppsn_low_mutation)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha[ startsWith( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$work, "base-"),]$work <- "Base"
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha[ !startsWith( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$work, "Base"),]$work <- "Work"

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp <- comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp_1 + comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$initial_temp_2 / 2

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp <- comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp_1 + comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha$final_temp_2 / 2

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates <- process_covariates( comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha )

no_alpha_plot <- ggplot(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates, aes(x = diff_fitness, y = PKG, color=interaction(group,alpha),shape=factor(alpha) )) +
  geom_point(alpha=0.5) +
  scale_x_log10()+
  labs(x = "Diff Fitness",
    y = "PKG Energy"
  ) + theme(legend.position = "bottom") + theme_minimal()

ggMarginal(no_alpha_plot, type="density", groupFill=T, alpha=0.5)
@

The achieved fitness, however, can probably be improved, so we will try to perform an additional parametric improvement, taking into account that the $\alpha$ percentage has a significant influence on the energy spent, as well as the fitness achieved: we will suppress mutation for this {\em caste}, leaving it to reproduce only via crossover. Additionally, we will differentiate the $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ caste, giving the last one a 20\% probability of changing an element, that is, 2 elements changed for every individual; we will call this configuration {\sf no\_alpha}. What we see in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.cero.alpha} is a PKG (in J) vs. {\sf diff\_fitness} plot that uses different colors for the two groups interacting with the $\alpha$ percentage.

It is interesting to see that there is not a large difference between the two $\alpha$ values in the {\sf low\_mutation} experiment; however, eliminating mutation makes a difference in fitness, with a higher percentage leading to lower fitness values. But what is more important here is that there are no big differences in energy spent. We will try to dig into the model to effectively check if we have finally achieved a free lunch.

<<ppsn.no.alpha.model, message=FALSE, echo=FALSE, warning=F, fig.cap="Violin boxplot, no-alpha vs. low-mutation.", fig.height=3, fig.pos="h!tbp">>=
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$alpha <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$alpha)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$steps <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$steps)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$max_gens <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$max_gens)
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$population_size <- factor(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$population_size)

no_alpha_evaluations_model <- glm( evaluations ~ group*alpha + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_evaluations <- residuals( no_alpha_evaluations_model )

no_alpha_initial_temp_model <- glm( initial_temp ~ group + seconds, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_initial_temp <- residuals( no_alpha_initial_temp_model )

no_alpha_seconds_model <- glm( seconds ~ group + initial_temp + evaluations + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )
comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$residual_seconds <- residuals( no_alpha_seconds_model )

no_alpha_energy_model <- glm( PKG ~ alpha*group + PKG_baseline_prev + PKG_baseline_post + residual_initial_temp + residual_seconds + residual_evaluations + steps*max_gens*population_size, data = comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates )

anova_no_alpha_energy_model <- anova( no_alpha_energy_model )

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates %>% group_by( population_size, steps, max_gens, alpha, group ) %>%
  summarise( mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness),
             trim_mean_diff_fitness = mean(diff_fitness, trim=0.2),
             mean_PKG = mean(PKG),
             trim_mean_PKG = mean(PKG, trim=0.2),
             mean_evaluations = mean(evaluations),
             mean_initial_temp = mean(initial_temp),
             mean_residual_seconds = mean(residual_seconds) ) -> summary_comparison_no_alpha

energy_effort_summary <- comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates %>%
  # 1. Create a bin for the log10 fitness (e.g., rounds -1.2 to -1)
  # You can use round() or floor() depending on how you want to capture the interval
  mutate(fitness_level = round(log10(diff_fitness))) %>%

  # 2. Filter only for the target fitness levels (-1 through -6)
  filter(fitness_level %in% c(-1, -2, -3, -4, -5, -6)) %>%

  # 3. Group by your experimental group and the new fitness level
  group_by(fitness_level,group) %>%

  # 4. Summarize the PKG (energy) to find the effort required
  summarise(
    median_effort = median(PKG, na.rm = TRUE), # Median is usually safer with scatter outliers
    mean_effort   = mean(PKG, na.rm = TRUE),
    min_effort    = min(PKG, na.rm = TRUE),    # The absolute lowest energy used to hit this level
    n_samples     = n(),                       # Good to know how many data points are in this bin
    .groups       = "drop"
  ) %>%

  # 5. (Optional) Sort it so it reads cleanly
  arrange(-fitness_level, desc(group))

comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$fitness_level = round(log10(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates$diff_fitness))

ggplot(comparison_low_mutation_no_alpha_covariates, aes(x = factor(-fitness_level), y = PKG, fill = group)) +
  geom_violin() +
  labs(x = "Fitness Level (negative log10 scale)", y = "Median Energy (PKG in Joules)", fill = "Experimental Group") +
  theme_minimal()
@

We have plotted in Figure \ref{fig:ppsn.no.alpha.model} the energy spent per configuration vs. the fitness level reached, where the latter is the integer part of the logarithm of {\sf diff\_fitness}. The chart explains why {\sf low\_mutation} is spending less energy: when it ends with a relatively bad fitness level, the energy is distributed around the 350-400 J level. It does not change when the -1 level is reached; in order to reach that level, which is the minimum for {\sf no\_alpha}, the latter needs to spend a bit more energy. However already at the -2 level we see that the latter needs less energy, and that the energy ranges changes relatively little to reach deeper energy levels; summarizing, {\sf low\_energy} spends less energy just because it is stopping after a relatively low number of evaluations at a shallow fitness level; when comparable levels of fitness are reached, {\sf no\_alpha} is, indeed, a winner.

We have thus found a very economical lunch indeed, which can even be free if we take into account that the energy needed by {\sf no\_alpha} to reach the -3 level is lower than the energy needed by {\sf low\_mutation} to reach the level above, -2.

\section{Discussion and conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}


